Z||000000||theogre||05-10-2001||08:28 PM||||Several notes in the past have mentioned fluid leaking from the "rubber plug" in the middle of the rear caliper psitons....<P>Here's a few things I recently dicovered about the "plug."<P>1. It's not a plug. It's a bidirectional air valve.<P>2. It Does Not recieve pressuized brake fluid.<P>3. If the plug is leaking fluid then the Pistons must be replaced. Such a leak indicates a seal failure that Can Not be fixed with common tools. The seal is inside the piston assebly.<P>4. The plug should be CAREFULLY removed and cleaned with brake parts cleaner if it is blocked with dirt.<P>5. DO NOT coat the plug with grease! Do Not Ever use "anti squeal" compound on the inboard pad!<P>The job of this valve is to equalize air pressure between the TWO pistons in the cailper. If the plug is blocked then you could have air enter hydraulic section of the rear calipers.<P>Eventually I'll have an addition to the cave brake article. This should give you an idea.<P>------------------<BR><I><B>Screamin Yellow Zonkers... If it's Screamin and Yellow, I ain't eatin it.</B></I>||||reg||1
